enses": [{"gloss": "1. Indrota is twice mentioned in the Rigveda in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Liberality’) as a giver of gifts. In the second passage he has the epithet ātithigva, which shows conclusively that he was a son of atithigva, as Ludwig holds, and not of ṛkṣa, as Roth states.\nFoot notes viii. 68, 10 et seq.\nTranslation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.\nSt. Petersburg Dictionary, s.v.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["indrota", "indrota_1", "इन्द्रोत", "ఇన్ద్రోత", "ఇన్ద్రోత_౧", "ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ", "ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ_೧"], "synonyms_slp1": ["indrota", "indrota_1", "indrota", "ఇన్ద్రోత", "ఇన్ద్రోత_౧", "ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ", "ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ_೧"]}], "indrota dEvapa sOnaka": [{"id": "indrota dEvApa SOnaka", "headword": "इन्द्रोत दैवाप शौनक", "headword_slp1": "indrota dEvApa SOnaka", "fold": "indrota dEvapa sOnaka", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "2. Indrota daivāpa Śaunaka is mentioned in the Śatapatha brāhmaṇa as the priest who officiated at the horse sacrifice of janamejaya, although this honour is attributed in the aitareya brāhmaṇa to tura kāvaṣeya. He also appears in the Jaiminīya upaniṣad brāhmaṇa as a pupil of śruta, and is mentioned in the vaṃśa brāhmaṇa. He cannot be connected in any way with Devāpi, who occurs in the Rigveda.\nFoot notes xiii. 5, 3, 5; 4, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Srauta sūtra, xvi. 7, 7; 8, 27.\nviii. 21.\niii. 40, 1.\nIndische Studien, 4, 384, 385.\nx. 98. Cf. Oldenberg, Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft, 42, 240.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["indrota daivaapa shaunaka", "indrota daivaapa shaunaka_2", "indrota daivāpa śaunaka", "indrota daivāpa śaunaka_2", "इन्द्रोत दैवाप शौनक", "ఇన్ద్రోత దైవాప శౌనక", "ఇన్ద్రోత దైవాప శౌనక_౨", "ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ ದೈವಾಪ ಶೌನಕ", "ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ ದೈವಾಪ ಶೌನಕ_೨"], "synonyms_slp1": ["indrota daivaapa shaunaka", "indrota daivaapa shaunaka_2", "indrota daivāpa śaunaka", "indrota daivāpa śaunaka_2", "indrota dEvApa SOnaka", "ఇన్ద్రోత దైవాప శౌనక", "ఇన్ద్రోత దైవాప శౌనక_౨", "ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ ದೈವಾ